From the first law of thermodynamics, if an amount of heat $Q$ is given to a system, a part of it is used in increasing the internal energy $\Delta U$ of the system and the rest in doing work $W$ by the system.
$\begin{array}{rlrl}
\therefore & Q & =\Delta U+W \\
\Rightarrow & & \Delta U & =Q-W \Rightarrow \Delta U=150-110 \\
& & =40 \mathrm{ J}
\end{array}$

Net work done in cyclic process A B C A$,
$W_${\text{net }}$=$Areu enclosed by path A B C A
\begin{aligned} =\frac{1}{2} \times \left(3 V_{1}-V_{1}\right)\left(4 p_{1}-p_{1}\right)\\
 =\frac{1}{2} \times2 V_{1} \times3 p_{1}=3 p_{1} V_{1}
\end{aligned}

Since, cyclic process is taken$ in anti-clockwise direction, hence net work done will be negative. W_{$\text{net }$ }=-3 p_{1} V_{1}

Given, $T_{1}=227^{{}^{\circ}} \mathrm{C}=227+273=500 \mathrm{ K}$
T_{2}=127^{{}^{\circ}} $\mathrm{C}=127+273=400 \mathrm{ K}$ Heat input, $H_{i}=6 \times 10^{4} \mathrm{ J}$
Efficiency of Carnot engine, $\begin{aligned}
\eta$&=1-$\frac{T_{2}}{T_{1}}$\\
&=1-$\frac{400}{500}=\frac{1}{5}
\end{aligned}$ Also, efficiency, $\begin{aligned}
\eta$&=$\frac{\text{ Work output }}{\text{ Heat input }}=\frac{W}{6 \times 10^{4}}$\\
\Rightarrow \quad W &=\eta $\times6 \times10^{4}=\frac{1}{5} \times6 \times10^{4} \\ \text{$ and $} =1.2 \times10^{4} \mathrm{ J}
\end{aligned}$
